1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Arthur Forbes, 1st Earl of Granard:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p15262.htm#i152615
2 CONT
2 CONT Arthur Forbes, 1st Earl of Granard [1]
2 CONT *M, #152615, 
2 CONT *b. circa 1623, 
2 CONT *d. 1695
2 CONT *Last Edited=10 Apr 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Arthur Forbes, 1st Earl of Granard was born circa 1623.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Catherine Newcomen, daughter of Sir Robert Newcomen, 4th Bt. and Anne Boleyn, circa 1655.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1695.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Sir Arthur Forbes, 1st Bt. and Jane Lauder.[2] 
2 CONT *He fought in the English Civil War, in Scotland, as a Royalist.[3] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 2nd Baronet Forbes, of Castle Forbes, Co. Longford, Ireland [N.S., 1628] on 14 April 1652.[3] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Commissioner of the Court of Claims [Ireland] in 1661.[3] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for County Tyrone [Ireland] between 1661 and 1666.[3] 
2 CONT *He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Ireland] in 1670.[3] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Marshal of the Army [Ireland] between 1670 and 1684.[3] 
2 CONT *He held the office of a Lord of Justice [Ireland] in 1671.[3] He held the office of a Lord of Justice [Ireland] from 1675 to 1676.[3] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 1st Baron Clanehugh, co. Longford [Ireland] on 22 November 1675.[3] 
2 CONT *He was created 1st Viscount of Granard, co. Longford [Ireland] on 22 November 1675.[3] 
2 CONT *He was Colonel of the 18th Royal Irish Foot between 1684 and 1686.[3] 
2 CONT *He was created 1st Earl of Granard [Ireland] on 30 December 1684.[3] 
2 CONT *He held the office of a Lord of Justice [Ireland] in 1684/85.[3] 
2 CONT *He retired from the military in 1689, with the rank of Lieutenant-General.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Arthur Forbes, 1st Earl of Granard and Catherine Newcomen
2 CONT *1. Lady Catherine Forbes+[4] d. 15 Jun 1743
2 CONT *2. unknown son Forbes [3]
2 CONT *3. unknown son Forbes [3]
2 CONT *4. unknown son Forbes [3]
2 CONT *5. unknown son Forbes [3]
2 CONT *6. Arthur Forbes, 2nd Earl of Granard+[1] b. c 1656, d. 24 Aug 1734
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources 
2 CONT # [http://books.google.com/books?id=5vE7AAAAMAAJ&lpg=PA264&ots=hAe2yhr7-O&dq=Frances%20Newcomen%20William%20Stewart&pg=PA264#v=onepage&q=Frances%20Newcomen%20William%20Stewart&f=false Descent and alliances of Croslegh] or Crossle,
2 CONC  or Crossley, of Scaitcliffe; and Coddington of Oldbridge; and Evans, of Eyton hall (Google eBook).  Priv. print., The De La More press, 1904 - 441 pages.  Page 264  "NEWCOMEN, of KENAGH - LINEAGE, lll."
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VI, page 55. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *2. [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VI, page 54.
2 CONT *3. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 1628. Hereinafter cited as Burke's 
2 CONC Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *4. [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 445.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Dictionary of the National Biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Forbes,_Arthur_(DNB00)
2 CONT
2 CONT FORBES, Sir ARTHUR, first Earl of Granard (1623–1696), eldest son of Sir Arthur Forbes of Corse in Aberdeenshire (who went to Ireland in 1620 with the Master of Forbes's regiment, of which he was lieutenant-colonel, and was grante
2 CONC d large estates in Leitrim and Longford by James I), by Jane, daughter of Sir Robert Lauder of the Isle of Bass, and widow of Sir Alexander Hamilton of Killeshandra, co. Cavan, a lady of singular ability and courage, was born in 1
2 CONC 623, and at an early age exhibited conspicuous spirit and ability. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was killed in a duel in 1632, and he was trained entirely under his mother's care. During the rebellion of 1641 she was besieged in Castle Forbes, the family seat, for nine months, and Forbes raised men for her relief, 
2 CONC though only 18 years old. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is next heard of in Scotland, serving under Montrose in the cause of Charles I. On the defeat of Montrose in 1645 he was taken prisoner, and for two years confined in Edinburgh Castle. On his release he still embraced every opp
2 CONC ortunity to aid the fallen fortunes of the Stuarts, but, all efforts to restore them failing, he returned to Ireland in 1655. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1660 he was sent to Charles at Breda to assure him that if he would only go over to Ireland the whole kingdom would declare for him. At the Restoration he was appointed a commissioner of the court of claims in Ireland, and rece
2 CONC ived additional grants of land in Westmeath. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1661 he entered parliament as member for the family borough of Mullingar. In 1663 he did good service in the north of Ireland by nipping in the bud efforts there in support of Blood's plot. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Honours now flowed rapidly in on him. In 1670 he was sworn of the Irish privy council, and appointed marshal and commander-in-chief of the army. In 1671 he was one of the lords justices. On several subsequent occasions he held the
2 CONC  same post. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1672 he was the means of rendering to the presbyterian church of Ireland, of which he was an attached member, an important service, by procuring for it the first grant of regium donum, which that body continued to enjoy until t
2 CONC he passing of the Irish Church Act in 1869, with the exception of a short interval. Kirkpatrick, in his ‘Presbyterian Loyalty,’ gives an account of his action in this matter, which, he says, came ‘from Sir Arthur Forbes's own mout
2 CONC h,’ to the effect that he (Forbes) being in London, the king inquired of him as to the welfare of the Irish presbyterian ministers, of whose loyalty and sufferings in his cause he had often heard. Forbes having told him that ‘they
2 CONC  lived in no great plenty,’ the king said ‘that there was 1,200l. a year in the settlement of the revenue of Ireland which he had not yet disposed of, but designed it for a charitable use, and he knew not how to dispose of it bett
2 CONC er than by giving it to these ministers.’ It subsequently appeared that only 600l. was available for the purpose, and at this figure the grant was made to Forbes (Presbyterian Loyalty, p. 384).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1675 he was created Baron Clanehugh and Viscount Granard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1684 he raised the 18th regiment of foot, and was made colonel thereof, and in the same year was advanced to the dignity of Earl of Granard. James II, when he came to the throne, endeavoured to make use of his services for the 
2 CONC promotion of the interests of Romanism, but Granard could not be induced to betray his fellow-protestants. He was accordingly removed from the command of the army, Tyrconnel being put in his place. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When James's Dublin parliament passed the acts of repeal and attainder, he boldly remonstrated with the king. Finding his arguments vain, he went to the House of Lords, entered his solemn protest against these measures, and retire
2 CONC d to Castle Forbes. Here he was besieged by the Irish, but in vain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When William went over to Ireland, no one welcomed him more heartily than Granard. He was placed by the king in command of a force of five thousand men for the reduction of Sligo, the surrender of which he secured. This was his la
2 CONC st public service. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His closing years were spent quietly at Castle Forbes, where he died in 1696.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Catherine, daughter of Sir Robert Newcomen of Mosstown, co. Longford, and widow of Sir Alexander Stewart, ancestor of the Mountjoy family, by whom he had five sons and one daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT *[Forbes's Memoirs of the Earls of Granard; 
2 CONT *Kirkpatrick's Historical Essay upon the Loyalty of Presbyterians; 
2 CONT *Adair's True Narrative; 
2 CONT *Reid's History of the Presbyterian Church in Ireland.]
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * Wikipedia contributors. "<em>[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Forbes,_1st_Earl_of_Granard Arthur Forbes, 1st Earl of Granard]</em>." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia.
2 CONT * Burke, Bernard, Sir. [http://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhera00inburk#page/508/mode/2up A genealogical and heraldic dictionary of the peerage and baronetage of the British Empire]. London : Harrison 1869. page 509
