2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Stewart,_2nd_Lord_Ochiltree '''Wikipedia''' (Andrew Stewart, 2nd Lord Orchitree - kids.  ]) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Working links 2026:
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.nashultshembygd.se/nashultsbor/alexander-stuart-de-skotska-kungarnas-frande-i-odmundetorp/ Alexander Stuart, de skotska kungarnas frände i Ödmundetorp]
2 CONT * [https://wallbring.se/Bouppteckningar/david%20stuarts%20utsagor Wallbring]
2 CONT * [https://familjenbostrom.se/genealogi/norrbotten/Artiklar-Ulf/henning.html Boström]
2 CONT * [https://www.stewartsociety.org/history-of-the-stewarts.cfm?section=famous-stewarts&subcatid=18&histid=3566 Stewart Society] (even St. Andrews is right sometimes)
2 CONT * [https://kids.kiddle.co/Andrew_Stewart,_2nd_Lord_Ochiltree Facts for Neils]
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Stuart Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===ANDREW STUART OF STARSATER (aka Anders Stuart of Starsäter)===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Written almost correctly. Typos always creates trust.
2 CONT * [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0073910_00264#?c=&m=&s=&cv=263&xywh=-54%2C580%2C5945%2C3165 Riksarkivet]
2 CONT * [https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anders_Stuart_den_%C3%A4ldre Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT # [http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Stuart_nr_86#TAB_43 Adelsvapen-Wiki: Stuart nr 86 tab 43]
2 CONT # [https://www.st-andrews.ac.uk/history/ssne/item.php?id=1646 University of St. Andrews: Institute of Scottish Historical Research]
2 CONT
2 CONT * St. Andrews is really not a solid source. It does not provide anything original, it only parrots Adelsvapen with shortcomings and all.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Stuart_nr_86#TAB_43 BIOGRAPHICAL SUMMARY]
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Stuart of Starsäter (c.1570-1640), military commander, diplomat and Swedish nobleman, was born in Scotland the son of John Stewart of Ochiltree and Agneta Forbes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources note Andrew as the grandson of Stewart of Ochiltree and thus as the son of John/Hans [SSNE 1645] who had also migrated to Sweden and gained distinction as a Gentleman of the Bedchamber at the Court of Karl IX in 1604 
2 CONC and Inspector-general of all foreign troops in Swedish service by 1609. The two Stuarts are sometimes confused in source material. Their genealogy is interesting in that no John Stewart of Ochiltree can be traced in the Scottish p
2 CONC eerage. However, Andrew’s godfather was Andrew Stewart 2nd Lord Ochiltree, who signed a letter proving Hans Stuart’s nobility in 1585. This was accompanied by an open letter from James VI of Scotland that referred to Hans as “our 
2 CONC dear relative”. These documents allowed Hans Stuart to use the same coat of arms as James Stuart Earl of Arran, second son of Lord Ochiltree suggesting a relationship which remains unclear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Stuart immigrated to Sweden and became a Gentleman of the Bedchamber at Duke Karl (later Karl IX) of Södermanland’s court by 1592. An Andrew Stuart was a captain on the ship Svenska Bjorn in Joakim Scheel's fleet in 1598. H
2 CONC e was known in Sweden as ‘Anders the elder’ to distinguish him from his nephew who also served in the Swedish army. In reward for his military services, Andrew the elder received land in 1599 from Karl IX in Ledingelunda/Lagerlund
2 CONC , part of the parish of Kärna in Sweden. During this period he also married Elisabet Anrep, daughter of Adolf Anrep and Gertrud von Nieroth although the exact date remains unclear.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the beginning of 1600 Stuart became a commander of the Swedish forces at Fellin in Livland and by 1602 he served as governor of Dorpat. His administrative duties continued when in 1604 he assumed responsibility for the Norra Mö
2 CONC re region of Kalmar. He later received further lands in Starsäter (renamed Stuartsäter) in Vists parish and Lida in Kvillinge parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart continued to serve in the army and became colonel in chief for the Östgöta infantry in 1605 and was present at the battle of Kirkholm on 17 September. However, he continued to pursue work outside his military life.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Kalmar War between Sweden and Denmark-Norway (1611-1613), Stuart joined the Swedish diplomatic service and acted as their envoy to Russia in 1611. During this period Stuart also maintained contact with the Court of Jame
2 CONC s VI and I. Perhaps more surprising is that Stuart also had indirect communication with Christian IV of Denmark-Norway through a Scottish nobleman in Danish service, Andrew Sinclair, governor of the newly conquered Swedish fortres
2 CONC s of Kalmar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart’s functions in later life included service in the Swedish fleet. He is recorded as having become a Vice-Admiral [some sources say Rear-Admiral] in the Riksamiral’s (state admiral) fleet in 1621. He also continued to hold po
2 CONC sts on land, acting as Master of the Court to Pfalzgreve Johan Casimir in 1633. During his life, Stuart also acted as Governor-General of Livonia for the Swedes. This is possibly the vice-admiral Stewart noted as engaging in troop
2 CONC -transport operations in 1638 from Finland to Usedom. He died on 27 December 1640 apparently from natural causes, although he was still listed as in service as a vice admiral of the navy in 1641 (this is presumably confusion with 
2 CONC Simon Stewart [SSNE 1644]). At some point he was ennobled in Sweden. Together Stuart and his wife Elisabet had three sons and two daughters: Carl (c.1600-1637), Johan Adolf (1603-1666) [SSNE 3623], Anders (?-1637)[SSNE 3611], Chri
2 CONC stina (1600-1648) [SSNE 6251] and Catharina (1622-1699) [SSNE 6254]. The sons all engaged in Swedish military service and both daughters also married soldiers, Christina to Herbert Gladstone and Catharina to Anders Rosenstrale.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Wiki translation of the Swedish Wiki==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anders Stuart''' was born in Scotland. He was the son of Johannes Stuart and Agneta* Forbes, and the brother of Hans Stuart. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Agneta. Really? That's a Swedish name, not Scottish. "Jean" has turned to Agneta. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart moved to Sweden in 1592 and became a chamberlain and courtier to Duke Karl. On 10 December 1599, Duke Karl granted him the estate of Lagerlunda in Kärna parish, which had been forfeited by Töres Haraldsson (Stålhandske). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He became commander at Fellin in Livonia on 19 November 1600 and governor of Dorpat in 1602. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart became district judge of Norra Möre district on 16 June 1604 and colonel of the Östgöta infantry on 29 July 1605. In 1611 he was sent to Russia and in 1633 became chamberlain to Count Palatine Johan Kasimir of Pfalz-Zweibrü
2 CONC cken. Stuart died in 1640 and was buried in Vist Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1624 he was a pallbearer at the funeral of Princess Kristina Augusta. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Property===
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders Stuart owned the Stavsäter estate in Vist parish and the Lida estate in Kvillinge parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart married Elisabeth Anrep in Livonia, the daughter of Adolf Anrep and Gertrud von Nieroth. Together they had the following children: Carl Stuart; Commander Johan Adolf Stuart (1603–1666) at Johannisborg; Ensign Anders Stuart 
2 CONC (d.y.) (died 1637) in the Östgöta infantry; Christina Stuart, who was married to Colonel Herbert Gladtsten; and Catharina Stuart (1622–1699), who was married to Cornet Anders Rosenstråle.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Heraldic Analysis of the Stuart Coat of Arms==
2 CONT
2 CONT The image is a quarterly shield, which is a traditional way of combining several lineages or titles into a single coat of arms. It accurately reflects the complex history of the House of Stewart (Stuart).
2 CONT ​
2 CONT ===Breakdown of the Quarters===
2 CONT ​
2 CONT '''First Quarter''' (Top Left): This features the Royal Arms of Scotland. It displays a red lion rampant (standing on its hind legs) within a red "double tressure flory-counter-flory" (the decorative border with fleurs-de-lis) on 
2 CONC a gold field. This represents the family’s long reign as Kings and Queens of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ​'''Second Quarter''' (Top Right): This shows a gold field with a blue and silver checkered band (fess chequy). This is the original and most famous emblem of the High Stewards of Scotland. The checkered pattern is said to represe
2 CONC nt the cloth used by the early Stewards for counting money in their role as royal treasurers.
2 CONT ​
2 CONT '''Third Quarter''' (Bottom Left): This depicts a red diagonal cross (saltire) between four red roses on a silver field. This is the coat of arms of the House of Lennox. The Stuart of Lennox branch was a major cadet branch; notabl
2 CONC y, Lord Darnley (Mary Queen of Scots' husband) belonged to this line.
2 CONT ​
2 CONT '''Fourth Quarter''' (Bottom Right): This displays a gold lion rampant on a red field. This is the traditional arms of the Earldom of Fife, an ancient Scottish title that became linked to the Stewarts through marriage and inherita
2 CONC nce.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''What's in the middle?''' Three stars on a blue background (as in three Bears in the Forbes crest).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Conclusion===
2 CONT
2 CONT ​The image is a very faithful representation of a high-ranking member of the Stuart dynasty. Rather than being a simple family crest, it is a compositional shield that tells the story of the family's rise to royalty and their stra
2 CONC tegic alliances with other powerful Scottish noble houses.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Riksarkivets ämnessamlingar. Personhistoria https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0073910_00198#?c=&m=&s=&cv=197&xywh=2696%2C146%2C4106%2C2251
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0073910_00206#?c=&m=&s=&cv=205&xywh=2241%2C2475%2C3419%2C1874
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sok.riksarkivet.se/bildvisning/A0073910_00256#?c=&m=&s=&cv=255&xywh=1441%2C1628%2C4913%2C2693
2 CONT
2 CONT Swedish Riksarkiv, P. Sondén, Militärachefer i svenska arméen och deras skrivelser; Biografiska Anteckningar om Officerare vid Orlogsflottan 1600-1699, p.366; Meritforteckningar (Flottan); J. Berg and B. Lagercrantz, The Scots in 
2 CONC Sweden (Stockholm, 1962), pp.20-21; C.F. Bricka and J.A. Fridericia, Kong Christian den Fjerdes Egen-Haendige Breve, (8 vols., Copenhagen, 1878-1947), vol. 1, pp.63-65; Register till Sveriges Ridderskaps och Adels Riksdags-Protoko
2 CONC ll (17 vols, Stockholm, 1910), vols. for 1627-1640; G. Elgenstierna, Svenska Adelns Ättartavlor, vol. 7, p.793; Otto Donner, A brief sketch of the Scottish families in Finland and Sweden (Helsingfors, 1884), p.27; T. Fischer, The 
2 CONC Scots in Sweden (Edinburgh, 1907), p.176; A. Zettersten, Svenska Flottans Historia Åren 1522-1634 (Stockholm, 1890), pp.61-2; H. Borjeson and G. Hafström, Skeppshövidsman vid örlogsflottan under 1500-talet, biografiska anteckninga
2 CONC r, p.73.
