1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==='''Agnes ''Cunningham'' Stewart, Lady Ochiltree'''===
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Stewart,_2nd_Lord_Ochiltree?wprov=sfti1
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Stewart, 2nd Lord Ochiltree married Agnes Cunningham of the Caprington family. Their children, and grandchildren included;
2 CONT
2 CONT # Andrew Stewart, known as the Master of Ochiltree (d. 1578). He married Margaret Stewart (d. 1627), daughter of Henry Stewart, 1st Lord Methven, who was known as the Mistress of Ochiltree, and was a member of the households of An
2 CONC ne of Denmark and Prince Henry. Their children included;
2 CONT ## Andrew Stuart, 1st Baron Castle Stuart, who became the 3rd Lord Ochiltree.[citation needed]
2 CONT ## Josias Stewart of Bonington,[6] who was a supporter of and conspirator with the Francis Stewart, 5th Earl of Bothwell and in 1595 revealed information to the Privy Council of Scotland about a band or league between the Earl and
2 CONC  the Catholic rebel Northern earls.[7] He was the executor and administrator of Jean Stewart, Lady Bargany and her son Thomas Kennedy, see below.[8]
2 CONT ## Mary Stewart (d. 1606), who with her mother was a lady in waiting in the household of Anne of Denmark. She married Roger Aston, an English favourite of James VI who had appointed him Gentleman of the Bedchamber in 1587.
2 CONT ## Anne Stewart, who married Andrew Kerr, Lord of Jedburgh.
2 CONT ## Jean Stewart, also a maiden in the household of Anne of Denmark, married her cousin Gilbert Kennedy younger of Bargany. The wedding was celebrated at court with food and music and King James gave her clothes.[9] It was said tha
2 CONC t James VI compelled the Laird of Bargany to arrange the marriage without a dowry, because he had sided with kirk ministers against him. The queen arranged a loan for the dowry.[10]
2 CONT ## Margaret Stewart, also a maiden in the queen's household, who married in January 1596 Sir John Stewart younger of Traquair, a son of William Stewart of Caverston.[11]
2 CONT ## Martha Stewart, who married Nicholas Rutherford of Hundalee.
2 CONT # James Stewart of Bothwellmuir, who became Lord Chancellor of Scotland and Earl of Arran.
2 CONT # William Stewart of Monkton, Ayrshire,[12] Provost of Ayr in 1585, murdered by Francis Stewart, 5th Earl of Bothwell in July 1588.
2 CONT # Henry Stewart of Braidwood.[13][14]
2 CONT # Margaret Stewart, who married John Knox on Palm Sunday 1564, aged around sixteen (Knox was around 60).[15] The English diplomat in Scotland, Thomas Randolph noted that she was a near kinswoman of James Hamilton, Duke of Châtelle
2 CONC rault, Andrew's mother was the Duke's half-sister. Randolph thought that Cecil would think his report of the marriage madness.[16] They were married on Palm Sunday after the banns had been proclaimed in St Giles, Edinburgh. Randol
2 CONC ph wrote that the Queen was angry at the marriage because Margaret was of the royal "blood and name".[17] After Knox died she married Andrew Ker of Faldonside.[18]
2 CONT # Isobel Stewart, married Sir Thomas Kennedy of Bargany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Stewart, 2nd Lord Ochiltree married secondly, Margaret Cunningham, daughter of Alexander Cunningham, 5th Earl of Glencairn, and widow of John Wallace of Craigie.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/512/mode/2up '''The Scots Peerage'''], Vol. 6, page 512-514.
