3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE JUL 1588
1 OCCU Courtier 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Stewart of Monkton and Carstairs''' (died 1588) was a Scottish landowner and courtier.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Stewart_of_Monkton
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son of Andrew Stewart, 2nd Lord Ochiltree and Agnes Cunningham. His elder brother was James Stewart, Earl of Arran and his sister Margaret Stewart married John Knox.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the English diplomats Thomas Randolph and Edward Hoby, he was known as "William the Sticker."[1] His estates were at Monkton, Ayrshire and Carstairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart was keeping custody of Lord Maxwell in Robert Gourlay's house in Edinburgh, when Francis Stewart, 5th Earl of Bothwell killed him on Blackfriar's Wynd in July 1588.[8] Bothwell stabbed him with his rapier, and Stewart ran 
2 CONC and tried to hide in a cellar, where Bothwell's men "stobbed him with whingers till he was despatched.[9] The murder followed a quarrel in the king's presence in which, according to David Calderwood, Stewart asked to Bothwell to k
2 CONC iss him behind.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Helen Cunningham. Their children included a son William, and a daughter Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son William Stewart killed James Douglas, Lord Torthorwald, a son of George Douglas of Parkhead, in Edinburgh on 14 July 1608, in revenge for the murder of his uncle James Stewart, Earl of Arran.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
