1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == THOMAS MAKDOWELL ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Thomas Makdowell, here treated, is the brother of https://www.geni.com/people/James-Makdowell-of-Mackerstoun/6000000177142330821?through=6000000008027059733 [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?re
2 CONC ference=RH6%2f2645&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=makdowall&ko=p&r=&ro=m&df=&dt=1600&di=n NRS: RH6/2645]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == His Father's Last Will and Testament ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Thomas Mcdowell of Mackerstoun died in April 1571. He made his last will and testament at Makerstoun on 24 April 1571 and died  before 30 April 1571. He nominated and appointed his sons James and Thomas to be his executors test
2 CONC amentor and intromitters with his goods and gear.. He left his part of his goods and gear (the dead's part) to his son Thomas and Richard. Confirmation was granted to the executors testamentar on 27 February 1576. [National Record
2 CONC s of Scotland, Edinburgh Commissary Court, The Testament Testamentar and Inventory of Thomas Makdowell of Makcarston, reference CC8/8/4]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000206735699826 Image of Testament]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000206735508831 Image of Testament]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000206735371846 Image of Testament]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''14 November 1582: Contract of Marriage betwixt JAMES MAKDOUGALL (who signs himself Makdowall) OF MAKAIRSTOUN and THOMAS MAKDOUGALL, his son and apparent heir on one part and Alexander Home of Huttounhall and Margaret Home, his 
2 CONC daughter lawful on the other, Whereby said Thomas was to marry said Margaret betwixt and Festrounsevin next to come (12th January 1582/3). It is provided inter alia that the said James before the completion of the marriage is to i
2 CONC nfeft said Thomas and Margaret and the survivor of them in conjunct fee and the heirs male to be begotten betwixt them, whom failing, said Thomas' heirs male whomsoever, in the town and lands of Danyealtoun lying in the lordship a
2 CONC nd regality of Melrose, and in the three husbandlands with the onstead lying in the town and territory of Makairstoun which was possessed before by THOMAS MAKDOUGALL, his brother, also before Easter next to come (31st March 1583) 
2 CONC to infeft said Thomas his son, and his heirs male whomsoever bearing the surname of Makdougall and arms of the house of Makairstoun, in all his old lands and heritages viz the lands and barony of Makairstoun with the kirklands the
2 CONC reof, with the said town and lands of Danyealtoun, all lying within the sheriffdom of Roxburgh, to be held of James' immediate superiors, reserving to said James the liferent of said barony; and said James with consent of Isobel K
2 CONC err, his spouse, conjunct fiar of Wester Meirdene, was immediately after the completion of the marriage to put said Thomas and Margaret in possession of said lands, lying within said barony, to be brooked by them for their honoura
2 CONC ble living and to deliver to them 16 oxen and 2 maigis then being on said lands together with all corn being within the barn and barnyard thereof threshed as unthreshed, which grew on said lands of crop and year 1582 (the corn of 
2 CONC previous years to appertain to said James). Said Thomas and Margaret not to intromit with any fermes of Danyealtoun so long as they should possess said lands of Meirdene. Said James to pay to said Thomas and Margaret for their fur
2 CONC ther sustentation, two chalders bear yearly during his lifetime and said Alexander to pay them likewise two chalders victual, half wheat half meal, yearly during said space and cause carry the same to their dwelling house. If ther
2 CONC e should be no sons of the marriage but only daughters said James and Thomas bound themselves and their successors in the estates to pay to said daughters, if only one, 3000 merks; if two, to the elder 2000 merks, to the younger 1
2 CONC 000 merks; if more than two, the said sum to be distributed amongst them at the discretion of said Margaret's father and his heirs. For which causes said Alexander bound himself to pay to said James in name of tocher good, the sum
2 CONC  of 4000 merks, by instalments as specified. Both parties consented to registration in the Books of Council. At Edinburgh, 14th November 1582. Witnesses John Young, writer, James Home, brother to James Home of Carraldsyde, Richard
2 CONC  Meik, servant to said James, Thomas Dikesoun, servant to said Alexander, Thomas Middilmaist, James Tarbert, notary, with others divers. Signed by the parties except the lady; also by John Young, James Hume, James Tarbert and Will
2 CONC iam Makdowell, witnesses. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=RH6%2f2645&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=makdowall&ko=p&r=&ro=m&df=&dt=1600&di=n National Records of Scotland, Register House cha
2 CONC rters, 1st series, reference RH6/2645]'''
