1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''GEORGE NISBET of that ilk, second son of Philip Nisbet (III) succeeded his brother Adam (IV). George Nisbet was a member of the Parliament held at Edinburgh I 560-6 r, at which the authority of the Pope over Sco
2 CONC tland and the jurisdiction of Catholic Bishops was overthrown. He married in 1551 Elizabeth Cranstoun, daughter of Cuthbert Cranstoun of Thirlestane Mains and Isobel! Hume, his spouse. Six sons and one daughter were born of this m
2 CONC arriage. We are concerned only with Philip, the eldest son. George Nisbet  of that ilk (V) died on 14th January 1600, survived by his wife. He was succeeded by his eldest son, Sir Philip Nisbet (VI). [http://www.nesbittnisbet.org.
2 CONC uk/uploads/1/7/7/1/17711127/nesbit_of_that_ilk_g004872.pdf Nisbet of that Ilk. By Robert Chancellor Nesbitt: page 7]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===GEORGE NESBIT OF THAT ILK===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''31 May 1555: Charter by which George Nesbit of that Ilk confirms that he has given to Elizabeth Cranstoun in liferent, the lands of Mungoiswallis, with the castle and manor thereof, the mill anf town of West Nesbit, and the lan
2 CONC ds of Otterburn in the sheriffdom of Berwick. His charter follows upon a contract of marriage that passed bwtween him and Elizabeth's father, Cuthbert Cranstoun of Mains (presumably Thirlestane Mains). This charter was confirmed u
2 CONC nder the Great Seal of Scotland on 1 June 1555. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi04scot/page/137/mode/1up RMS: 608]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Of that Ilk, he was a member of the Parliament held at Edinburgh in1560-1 at which the authority of the Pope over Scotland was overthrown. In 1551 he married Elizabeth Cranstoun, the daughter
