2 CONT Of that Ilk, granted arms in consequence of his marriage, in 1582 toElizabeth, the daughter of John Haldane of Gleneagles and ElizabethLundy. The Haldane family were closely linked by both marriage anddescent to the higher ranks o
2 CONC f the nobility and through them to theRoyal Line of Scotland. The Lundy or Lundin family was descended froma de London who was granted lands in Fife in the early Middle Ages.The male line failed and an heiress married a Maitland, 
2 CONC a subsequentheiressof the Maitland's of Lundin taking the estates to JohnDrummond, Earl of Melfort. <p></p> <p>Sir Philip was knighted in 1605, and was appointed a Commissioner torepresent Berwickshire in the Parliament of 1625. A
2 CONC  brother,Alexander, went to Ireland and married Alice, his cousin and thedaughter of the Rev. Alexander Cunningham, Deanof Raphoe, in 1630,and was founder of the families of Nesbitt of Woodhill, Tubberdaly,and Drumalee; and throug
2 CONC h Drumalee the family of Brenter, which becameLismore. A branch of this family moved to the City of London asmerchants in the mid18th Century. Sir Philip and Elizabeth had toursons and a daughter. Another younger son, Thomas, is n
2 CONC ext in thedescent leading to the Nesbitt's of Tullydonnell and Aughamore. <p></p> <p>Of that Ilk, granted arms in consequence of his marriage, in 1582 toElizabeth, the daughter of John Haldane of Gleneagles and ElizabethLundy. The
2 CONC  Haldane family were closely linked by both marriageanddescent to the higher ranks of the nobility and through them to theRoyal Line of Scotland. The Lundy or Lundin family was descended froma de London who was granted lands in Fi
2 CONC fe in the early MiddleAges.The male line failed and an heiress married a Maitland, a subsequentheiressof the Maitland's of Lundin taking the estates to JohnDrummond, Earl of Melfort. <p></p> <p>Sir Philip was knighted in 1605, and
2 CONC  was appointed a Commissioner torepresent Berwickshire in the Parliament of 1625. A brother,Alexander, went to Ireland and married Alice, his cousin and thedaughter of the Rev. Alexander Cunningham, Deanof Raphoe, in 1630,and was 
2 CONC founder of the families of Nesbitt of Woodhill, Tubberdaly,and Drumalee; and through Drumalee the family of Brenter, which becameLismore. A branch of this family moved to the City of London asmerchants in the mid18th Century. Sir 
2 CONC Philip and Elizabeth had toursons and a daughter. Another younger son, Thomas, is next in thedescent leading to the Nesbitt's of Tullydonnell and Aughamore.
