1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Anders Haraldsson Appelbom=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Harald Bengtsson and Ragnhild Jonsdotter, (Lind af Hageby)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Appelbom_nr_325#TAB_1 Noble family Appelbom no. 325, by Adelsvapen Geneologies]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''TABLE 1'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anders Haraldsson, ennobled Appelbom''' (oversight tab 3, son of Harald Bengtsson Tab III ), born 1576-05-12 in Nylödöse city, died 1649-08-27 in Söderby. District chief in Hölebo district. Married 1606-10-26 Partille to '''Car
2 CONC in Andersdotter Hök af Partille , daughter of the governor of Gullberg Anders Håkansson to Partille and Kerstin Arvidsdotter Kärling to Ravenäs .'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Kerstin Appelbom,''' born 1608-09-29 in Nylöse city, died 1691-10-23 in Uppsala. Married 1:o 1628 to the court preacher Aron Zynthius . Married 2nd in 1632 to Daniel Nikolai Sidenius, the assessor in the Svea Court of Appeal 
2 CONC
2 CONT * '''Carin Appelbom,''' born 17/12/1609 at Partille, died 22/09/1668 in Stockholm. Married 1631-08-14 to court councilor Johan Olofsson, ennobled Stiernhöök .
2 CONT * '''Ellika Appelbom,''' born 1611-03-25 in Partille, died 1697-02-21 in Kalmar. Married 1633-09-24 to Grels Gustafsson, the secretary in the chamber collegium .
2 CONT * '''Harald Appelbom,''' born 1612, died 1674. Councillor. See Tab. 2.
2 CONT * '''Anders Appelbom,''' born 1614, died 1686. County governor. Born 1614-01-07. County governor in Härnösand county. Died 13/10/1686. Married 1:o 1639-04-11 to Malin Andersdotter , daughter of the councilman in Stockholm Anders A
2 CONC ndersson and Ingrid Hansdotter Svan. Married 2nd to Catharina Gyldenbring , daughter of assessor Erik Bring, ennobled Gyldenbring and Catharina Edenberg .
2 CONT * '''Anna Appelbom,''' born 1615, died 1616.
2 CONT * '''Malin Appelbom,''' died 1632.
2 CONT * '''Arvid Appelbom.''' Died unmarried.
2 CONT * '''Mårten Appelbom.''' Killed in battle 1647.
2 CONT * '''Johan Appelbom.''' Died 1692. Major. born in Livland, died 1692-10-08. Major. Married 1:o 1650 to Anna Probsting from Elbingen, of a German noble family from Westphalia . Married 2nd in 1660 to Brigitta Crusebjörn , daughter 
2 CONC of the governor Frans Crusebjörn and Elisabet Skytte af Sätra .
2 CONT * '''Brita Appelbom,''' died 1689. Married 1644-01-09 in Stockholm to the ratepayer Jonas Persson Appelberg .
2 CONT * '''Samuel Appelbom.''' Died 1656. Lieutenant. Lieutenant at Västmanland's company of Uppland's horsemen. Died in April 1656 at the siege of Warschau. Married 1653-05-24 according to the king's permission to Maria Dress , daughte
2 CONC r of the lessee of the Västerjärnbergslagen André Dress (born from France) and Elisabet Schaeij (born from Holland).
2 CONT * '''Gustaf Appelbom.''' Captain in Bernhard von Gertten's regiment. Died in Ivangorod 09/11/1664. Married to Christina Dorotea von Fitinghoff in her 1st marriage, daughter of Captain Reinhold von Fitinghoff and NN von Schutz .
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://runeberg.org/strokirk/1/0241.html Cultural and personal history notes / First part / 237, (1915-1920) MARCH, by Author: Oscar Fredrik Strokirk]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Appelbom grave choir, in whose associated tomb vault under the church so many of the members of this family have received their final resting place, is located on the north side of the eastern gable of the church. It is surrou
2 CONC nded by a green wooden border and its vault is of soapstone. There is a tablet set up in it, with the family's coat of arms and Anders Haraldsson Appelbom's initials as well as two mourning stands and two swords: there are also tw
2 CONC o life-size oil portraits set up there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders Haraldsson Appelbom entered into marriage with Carin Andersdotter Hök af Partela or Partille near Gothenburg to Holmbonäs, died in 1631 and buried in Fellingsbro church. She was the daughter of Erik XIV's, Johan III's and C
2 CONC arl IX's chieftain over the people of West Göta and in 1569 governor of Gullberg fortress Anders Håkansson Hök and Kerstin Kärling to J.iavenäs in Toresund parish in Södermanland of old nobility. The Hök family to Partille receive
2 CONC d a coat of arms in 1545; it carried in its arms a grey hawk on a six-leafed twig in a white and blue field and on the open tonér helmet a similar hawk between two projecting lilies on their stems, each with seven leaves. Anders H
2 CONC araldsson Appelbom and his wife donated a metal chandelier with 12 candle pipes and the Appelbom coat of arms to Fellingsbro church . Above the coat of arms are his own initials and below those of his wife, namely KA D. (Karin And
2 CONC ersdotter). This chandelier is hung under the marisläktaren in the southern entrance to the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thirteen children were born to this marriage, six daughters and seven sons. Two of the daughters died at a young age, namely Anna Andersdotter, born in Stockholm in 1615, died in Söderby in 1615 and was buried in Fellingsbro churc
2 CONC h under a stone, on which she and her parents are mentioned and in which church a painted epitaph with an inscription to her memory was found. Malin Andersdotter died one year after her mother's death or in 1632. The other four da
2 CONC ughters entered into marriage. Kjerstin Andersdotter, born in 1608 in Nylöse town, died in 1691 in Uppsala, was first married in 1628 to the king, the court preacher Aron Zynthius, d. 1631, the second time in 1632 with the doctor 
2 CONC of law and professor of Romani law in Uppsala later assessor in the Svea court court Daniel Nicolai Sidenius, d. 1666, Catarina Andersdotter, b. 1609 in Partille, d. 1668, was married in 1631 to the court councilor and audit secta
2 CONC ry, the famous legal scholar Johan Olofsson Dalkarl, nobleman. Stjernhöök n :r 471, b. 1596 d. 1675.
2 CONT
2 CONT An obituary and personalia were printed about her in 1669, written by Nikolaus Rudbeckius. In this it is said, among other things: "that she was God-fearing, domestic, peaceful and far from all flamboyance." This funeral sermon is
2 CONC  included in the work of the herald Carl Arvid Klingspor published in 1877: "Sveriges adel under 1700- och 1800-talen". Ehlika Andersdotter to Högtorp was born in 1611 at Partille and died in 1697 in Kalmar and is buried there. Th
2 CONC e printed funeral sermon with the personal details about her was written by Olof Wallinus and mentions: "that she was pious, god-fearing and compassionate." Married in 1633 to the sectarian in the chamber college Grels Gustafsson 
2 CONC in his second marriage, died in 1640. Her two sons Anders and Mårten Grels-söner became noblemen, and baron Lindhjelm and Brita Afidersdotfer to Lillefyha and Wenagama in Ingermanland, died in 1689. In 1645 she married the rent ma
2 CONC ster in Narva and Ivangorod Jonas Persson
2 CONT
2 CONT Appelberg to Hessleberg and Hållika in Finland, b. 1609 d. 1672 in Narva. Their children were ennobled to Appelberg nr 904. The seven sons all reached maturity, but three of them left no descendants, namely Arvid, about whom we on
2 CONC ly know that he gave an oration "De Stratgematibus" in Uppsala in 1639. He was alive at the death of his father in 1649 and is mentioned in the dedication of his funeral sermon and died unmarried. Mårten, who was shot dead at Schw
2 CONC einfurth in Franconia in 1647 and is buried in the town of Nördlingen in Swabia north of the Danube, known from the Thirty Years' War for the defeat of the Swedish army there in 1634, and GustoJ, who in 1664 was captain in a drago
2 CONC on regiment and who was married to a von Fitinghoff; whether he left any children is not known. The other four were I. Harald, b. 1612 d. 1674, Swedish resident of the Dutch States General. II. Anders, b. 1614 d. 1686, county gove
2 CONC rnor in Hernösand, war chamber councilor. III. Johan, still alive in 1679, major in the Life Regiment. IV. Samuel, d. 1655, cavalry commander in the Life Regiment. Of these four branches of the family, only the youngest survives, 
2 CONC the one descended from Samuel. Harald's branch already went out with his son Carl. Anders' branch of the family lived longer or until 1815, but Johan's descendants became extinct in the middle of the 18th century with his grandchi
2 CONC ldren. I. Harald Appelbom to Forsmark in Roslagen was born in 1612 in Söderby and died in 1674 in The Hague. He was enrolled in 1631 under the name of Haraldus Andreæ at the University of Franeker and in 1640 took the name. Appelb
2 CONC om, under which his father Anders Haraldsson was ennobled in 1645. He entered the diplomatic career and in 1642 became correspondent and in 1647 commissioner in Amsterdam. In 1650 he successfully carried out an assignment, namely 
2 CONC to mediate the dispute between the Netherlands and Portugal in The Hague. Legate to the Parliament of England in 1652 to investigate English trade in Russia. Legate to the States General of the United Netherlands in the same year 
2 CONC and he offered them Sweden's mediation in the war against England. During the years 1653-1657 he was the city's highest governor; this dignity was abolished in 1719. He was appointed in 1657 as resident of the States General, in 1
2 CONC 663 as court councilor and extraordinary ordinary Abgesandt to the United Netherlands and as envoy in 1665. He was highly favored by King Charles II of England who said of him: "that he wanted to entrust his crown and scepter to h
2 CONC is disposition" (according to the Council Minutes of 1668).
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald Appelbom was one of the most distinguished diplomats that Sweden has ever had. His long diplomatic service, from 1652 to 1674, was spent almost entirely in Holland and only a short time in England. The external greatness of
2 CONC  Holland and Sweden began and developed at about the same time and ended for both at the beginning of the 18th century, when their position of power was disrupted and they were reduced to powers of the second order.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweden's pursuit of a "dominum maris baltici", i.e. dominion over the Baltic Sea, which was to become a Swedish inland lake, appeared openly during the time of Gustav II Adolf, only to be translated by Carl X Gustav into strong an
2 CONC d ruthless action for its realization. Holland's trading interests in the Baltic Sea were now threatened, which led to conflicts between Sweden and Holland, which could only be settled with difficulty by diplomatic means. Finally,
2 CONC  open feud broke out in 1658, when Carl X Gustaf attacked Denmark for the second time. Holland sided with Denmark and a strong Dutch fleet tried to make its way through the Sound to rescue Copenhagen. This was also successful afte
2 CONC r a glorious, although unfavorable battle for the Swedish fleet, at Kronborg, and Copenhagen was saved.
2 CONT
