2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ='''Agnes Keith, Countess of Moray'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes Keith, Countess of Moray (c. 1540 – 16 July 1588) was a Scottish noblewoman. She was the wife of James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray, regent of Scotland and the illegitimate half-brother of Mary, Queen of Scots, making her a si
2 CONC ster-in-law of the Scottish queen. As the wife of the regent, Agnes was the most powerful woman in Scotland from 1567 until her husband's assassination in 1570.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married secondly to Sir Colin Campbell, heir presumptive to the earldom of Argyll. When he succeeded his brother as the 6th earl in 1573, Agnes was henceforth styled Countess of Argyll. During her second marriage, Agnes be
2 CONC came embroiled in a litigation over Queen Mary's jewels which had earlier fallen into her keeping. It was her refusal to hand the jewels over to the Scottish Government that sparked a feud between the Earl of Argyll and the Regent
2 CONC  Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Family'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Agnes Keith[2][3] was born in Dunnottar Castle, Aberdeenshire, Scotland in about 1540, the eldest daughter of William Keith, 4th Earl Marischal, and Margaret Keith.[4] Her paternal grandparents were Robert Keith, Master of Ma
2 CONC rischal, and Lady Elizabeth Douglas, and her maternal grandparents were Sir William Keith and Janet Gray. Agnes was a descendant of King James I of Scotland and his consort Joan Beaufort, who was in her turn the great-granddaughte
2 CONC r of King Edward III of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dunnottar Castle''', the birthplace of Agnes Keith: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunnottar_Castle]
2 CONT
2 CONT She had two brothers, William Keith, Master of Marischal (died 1580), and Hon. Robert Keith, 1st Lord Altrie (died 1596); and six younger sisters. These were Elizabeth, wife of Sir Alexander Irvine of Drum; Alison, wife of Alexand
2 CONC er, Lord Salton; Mary, wife of Sir John Campbell of Calder; Beatrice, wife of John Allardice of Allardice; Janet, wife of James Crichton of Frendraght; and Margaret, wife of Sir John Kennedy of Balquhan.[5] Her aunt was Elizabeth 
2 CONC Keith, wife of George Gordon, 4th Earl of Huntly who would lead an unsuccessful rebellion against Mary, Queen of Scots in 1562. Her first cousin was Lady Jean Gordon, the first wife of James Hepburn, Earl of Bothwell, who himself 
2 CONC would become the third husband of Mary, Queen of Scots. Agnes's father was a member of Queen Mary's Privy Council; he had fought at the Battle of Pinkie when she was about seven years old. He died in 1581.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes was also known as Annabel[6][7] or Annas.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''First marriage'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT At St. Giles Cathedral, Edinburgh or at Holyrood on 8 February 1561/2,[9] Agnes was married to James Stewart, the illegitimate half-brother and chief adviser of Mary, Queen of Scots, who had been created Earl of Mar the previous d
2 CONC ay. The ceremony was magnificent, attended by many of the nobility, with John Knox having preached the sermon.[10] The lavish wedding was followed by three days of festivities and banqueting at Holyrood Palace, the frivolity of wh
2 CONC ich was subsequently denounced by Knox with the words: "the vanity used thereat offended many godly".[11] Queen Mary made much of the new Lady Mar and regarded her as a close member of her family.[12] Having been well-educated, Ag
2 CONC nes was described by author Antonia Fraser as having had "genuine intelligence and spirit".[13] Keith M. Brown, Professor of Scottish History at the University of St. Andrews, called her "clever, acquisitive and steely".[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes and her husband together had three daughters:[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Elizabeth Stewart, 2nd Countess of Moray''' (late 1565 – 18 November 1591), on 23 January 1581 married James Stewart of Doune, by whom she had five children including James Stewart, 3rd Earl of Moray.
2 CONT # '''Lady Annabell Stewart''' (1568/69 – 1570), according to the Diurnal of Occurents, Annabell was born at Stirling around 22 May 1568.[15]
2 CONT # '''Lady Margaret Stewart''', (born posthumously late January/18 April 1570 – before 3 August 1586), in 1584 married Francis Hay, 9th Earl of Errol. The marriage was childless.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT The queen had secretly given her half-brother the title of Earl of Moray in January 1562. This title belonged to George Gordon, 4th Earl of Huntly, himself married to Agnes' aunt, Elizabeth. James later agreed to give up the title
2 CONC  of Earl of Mar, it being an Erskine family perquisite, but retained the earldom of Moray. This provoked the Earl of Huntly to lead a rebellion in the Scottish Highlands against the queen. The rebellion was encouraged by Agnes's a
2 CONC unt. Huntly and his rebels were soundly defeated by James's troops at the Battle of Corrichie on 22 October 1562. Some of Huntly's forfeited belongings were sent to furnish the Morays' new castle of Darnaway.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl of Moray and his half-sister became enemies following Mary's marriage to Henry Stewart, Lord Darnley in 1565, a man to whom Moray was fiercely opposed. It does not appear, however, that his wife shared the same hostility 
2 CONC towards the queen.[18] Due to her advanced stage of pregnancy, Agnes was unable to join her husband, who was in exile in England. This was in consequence of Moray having been declared an outlaw following his rebellion, known as th
2 CONC e "Chaseabout Raid", against his sister in August that same year. Agnes remained behind at their home at St. Andrew's Priory; on an unknown date late in 1565, she gave birth to a daughter, Elizabeth. Upon recovery from her confine
2 CONC ment, she resumed the successful management of the Moray estates. Her husband returned to Scotland following the murder of Queen Mary's secretary David Rizzio in March 1566 and was pardoned by the queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was recorded that in August 1566 following the birth of Prince James, the future King James VI of Scotland, Agnes was one of the ladies with whom the queen kept the most company.[19] In early February 1567, Agnes suffered a mis
2 CONC carriage, which provided her husband with an excuse to hastily depart from Edinburgh; thus he was away when Lord Darnley was murdered at Kirk o'Field.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Most powerful woman in Scotland'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Mary was deposed by the Confederate Lords at the battle of Carberry Hill, while Moray was still in France. Mary was taken in custody to Lochleven Castle. Although the Lords would not forward Moray's letters to Mary, Agnes st
2 CONC ayed with the Queen and her mother-in-law at Lochleven in July 1567. The English ambassador in Edinburgh Nicholas Throckmorton heard there was "grete sorowe betwixt the Queen and her at theyre meeting and much gretter at theyre de
2 CONC parting."[21] Soon after on 24 July 1567, Mary abdicated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moray was proclaimed Regent of Scotland for the infant King James VI on 22 August 1567.[22] While her husband held the regency, Agnes, Countess of Moray was the most powerful woman in Scotland.[23] She was a very intelligent and i
2 CONC ntimidating politician,[24] and many people were afraid of incurring her wrath. In May 1568, before the Battle of Langside, she coldly informed her frightened cousin, George Gordon, 5th Earl of Huntly, "ye haf mad me angary".[24] 
2 CONC Huntly had indicated that he would support Mary rather than Regent Moray.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT Moray was assassinated at Linlithgow in January 1570,[26] by James Hamilton of Bothwellhaugh, a supporter of Queen Mary. Hamilton, using a pistol, fired at James from a window as the latter was passing in a cavalcade in the main s
2 CONC treet below, fatally wounding him. Agnes was pregnant at the time of her husband's murder and delivered a daughter, Margaret, shortly afterwards.[27] She spent the two years following his assassination managing the family estates 
2 CONC and fighting a series of legal battles in which she sought to obtain financial compensation for the time he acted as regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Agnes was at Dunnotar, her mother-in-law, Margaret Erskine, looked after her second eldest daughter, Annabell at the New House of Lochleven Castle. Although Annabell was described as "merry and very lusty" by Agnes' secretar
2 CONC y John Wood in April 1570, some months later Margaret had to write to the widowed Countess of Moray describing her death. She told Agnes that "God sall send your Ladyschip barnis efter this, for ye ar young aneuch."[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Second marriage and ex-communication'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 13 January 1571 and 26 February 1572, Agnes became the second wife of Sir Colin Campbell, the son of Archibald Campbell, 4th Earl of Argyll, and Lady Margaret Graham, and heir presumptive to the earldom of Argyll, by whom 
2 CONC she had another three children:[6][29]
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Hon. Colin Campbell of Lundie''' (died before 15 May 1619), married Maria Campbell, by whom he had issue.
2 CONT # '''Lady Jane Campbell''', married Sir Donald Campbell
2 CONT # '''Archibald Campbell, 7th Earl of Argyll''' (1575–1638), married firstly Lady Agnes Douglas by whom he had three children, including Archibald Campbell, 1st Marquess of Argyll; he married secondly Anne Cornwallis, by whom he ha
2 CONC d issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes was excommunicated by the Church on 25 April 1573 for non-adherence to her husband.[7] He was, in fact, said to have been "much advised by Agnes";[24] in another document it was recorded that Sir Colin was "overmuch ledd by 
2 CONC his wyf".[30]
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of her brother-in-law Archibald Campbell, 5th Earl of Argyll on 12 September 1573, Agnes, as the wife of Sir Colin who had succeeded his childless brother as the 6th earl, was henceforth styled as Countess of Argyll
2 CONC . It was noted by Professor Jane E. A. Dawson of the University of Edinburgh that Annas (Agnes) and her husband had been journeying to Darnaway Castle in Moray where they had planned to spend the winter when news reached them of t
2 CONC he 5th earl's death. They stopped instead at Dunnottar Castle and made alternative plans.[31]
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Queen Mary's Jewels'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Queen Mary was removed to Lochleven Castle in 1567 her jewels came into the Earl of Moray's custody. Moray sold some of the crown jewels to Elizabeth I of England and pledged others to fund the civil war.[32] Agnes obtai
2 CONC ned some of these jewels.[22] Mary, Queen of Scots wrote to Agnes from Tutbury Castle soon after Moray's assassination on 28 March 1570 regarding these jewels. Mary wanted them sent to her in England including a piece made up of d
2 CONC iamonds and rubies called the "H".[33] This was the "Great Harry", a diamond given to Mary on the occasion of her first marriage by her father-in-law, King Henry II of France.[34] The Earl of Huntly asked for the jewels on Mary's 
2 CONC behalf on 1 November 1570, and Mary herself wrote again for them on 27 January 1571. However, the Regent Lennox had also asked for them on 13 September 1570.[35] Facing a dilemma between handing the jewels over to Mary or the Scot
2 CONC tish government.[36] Agnes chose to hang onto the jewels.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was Agnes' desire to hold onto these valuable jewels which provoked a feud between her second husband and the Regent Morton,[23] who demanded their return on behalf of King James VI of Scotland, threatening the couple with arre
2 CONC st if they failed to deliver the jewels which he insisted belonged to the Scottish Crown. Agnes argued that she retained the jewels as a pledge for the debts owed to her for the expenses that the Earl of Moray had laid out as Rege
2 CONC nt of Scotland. When Agnes and her husband failed to hand them over, they were both "put to the horn" (declared rebels) on 3 February 1574.[37] Agnes appealed to the Scottish Parliament and wrote several articulate, formal letters
2 CONC  to Queen Elizabeth requesting her intervention which would permit Agnes to retain the jewels. These letters were considered by Francis Walsingham in September 1574.[38]
2 CONT
2 CONT The lengthy inquiry and litigation with Regent Morton over the custody of the precious stones,[39] ended on 5 March 1575, when the earl, in his own name and that of Agnes, surrendered them to Morton.[34] The Earl of Argyll would l
2 CONC ater be partly responsible for Regent Morton's fall from power and loss of the Regency in 1578.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes' second husband the 6th Earl of Argyll recorded that he was 'much advised by his wife' and she was considered an 'Intelligent and frightening politician.'[40]
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Death and legacy'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes died on 16 July 1588 in Edinburgh[41] and was buried in St. Giles Cathedral[6] inside the tomb of her first husband, James Stewart, Earl of Moray. The tomb is located in St. Anthony's aisle and was carved by John Roytell and
2 CONC  Murdoch Walker. Her will was probated on 9 August 1591.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Depiction in art'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Celebrated Flemish artist Hans Eworth painted portraits of Agnes and her first husband, the Earl of Moray in 1562 to commemorate their marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
