2 CONT
2 CONT DEACON CALEB FOBES FORBES
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Fobes-37
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 3 Jan 2021 | Created 30 Jul 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Caleb Fobes Sr (abt. 1655 - 1710)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Caleb Fobes Sr aka Forbes===
2 CONT
2 CONT Born about 1655 in Bridgewater, Plymouth, Massachusetts, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of John Fobes and Constant (Mitchell) Briggs
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Edward Fobes, William Fobes and Elizabeth (Forbes) Russell
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Mary Huntington — married [date unknown] [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Sarah (Gager) Fobes — married 30 Jun 1681 in Norwich, New London, Connecticut, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Sarah (Fobes) Bishop, Caleb Forbes, Mary (Fobes) Gates and John Fobes
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 25 Aug 1710 in Preston City, New London, Connecticut, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon Caleb Fobes of Norwich and Preston, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Frances Manwaring Caulkins in her History of Norwich, Connecticut refers to Caleb as "Forbes", I have yet to find a single instance in the town or church records where it is spelled with an "r". Generally, it is spelled f
2 CONC fobes. The double "ff" is used to distinguish it from "f" which is actually our modern "s".
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth:
2 CONT Caleb Fobes was the son of John and Constant (Mitchell) Fobes of Duxbury and Bridgewater, Mass., where it is assumed that he was born, about 1655, which seems to be the currently accepted date. I would put it a few years earlier, 
2 CONC because he owned land in Norwich in 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages 1st: Sarah Gager, daughter of John Gager of Norwich. They were married June 30, 1681 in Norwich.[1] Caleb is listed in his father-in-laws will dated 1695, although John Gager didn’t die until 1703. 2nd: Married Marah by 
2 CONC 1699. An entry in the Preston Church records establishes his marriage to Marah before Jan 1699. It lists Marah the wife of Caleb Fobes and others as having "adjoined by renewal of their covenant" on the 1st of Jan 1699.[2] It is b
2 CONC elieved by many researchers that the surname of Marah was Huntington.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: He died August 25, 1710 in Norwich.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Caleb was the owner of land in Norwich as early as 1672. In 1684 and 1685 he was constable east of the river. He was one of the petitioners for the incorporation of the town of Preston in Oct 1686. He is named among the fifteen la
2 CONC ndholders on the Plantation Act, dated Jan. 15, 1686 and became a settled inhabitant of the new town. He had several grants of land from Owaneco, chief of the Mohegans, one of which dated Dec 10, 1683, was for 110 acres of upland 
2 CONC and meadow “south of Connoughtug brook.”[4] He was one of the first twelve members and a deacon of the Preston Congregational Church. Caleb was frequently elected selectman or townsman held the office of town treasurer for many ye
2 CONC ars, and was often called upon to lay out lands and highways and to transact other public business.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death his estate was inventoried at £625. He left a widow, Marah/Mary, and five children, Sarah, Caleb, Mary, John, and Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children, so far as known, were
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah, b. in Norwich June 24, 1684;[1]< m. Samuel Bishop Jan. 2, 1705/6.[1] Caleb Fobes b. about 1688 (1728-40); d. May 7, 1728; m., May 21, 1713, Abigail Gates, who d. July 10, 1774, having borne (i) Abigail, b. May 25, 1714, (2)
2 CONC  Joshua, b. Sept. 22, 1715, (3) Sarah, b. Sept. 13, 1717, m. Zebulon Walbridge, (4) Caleb, b. June 20, 1719, m. Edith Walbridge, (5) Simon, b. Feb. 2, 1721-2, (6) Bethiah, b. Feb. 2, 1725-6.[5] Mary, m., June 6, 1716 Caleb Gates (
2 CONC both of Preston), and had ten children.[5] John Ffobes, d. Feb. 18, 1738/9, m., Jan. 14, 1718/9, Ruth Bruster (Brewster), had eleven children.[5] Elizabeth.[5] Because there are no records of Sarah's death, Caleb's marriage to Mar
2 CONC ah, or birth records for the children after Sarah, it is difficult to determine which of these children belong to which wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
