1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/515
2 CONT
2 CONT Above URL is the direct link to:  "The Scots Peerage VI: 515"   There is a reference and quote there on page 515 for the 4th son of Andrew, (not Lord but) Master of Ochiltree. The quote comes from the "General Registry of Inhibiti
2 CONC ons", xiii 250 as follows:  John Stewart, described as "brother german of Andrew, Lord Ochiltree, on 19 August, 1605." The General Registry of Inhibitions therefore confirms he lived past age 38. James/John's brother  Andrew the s
2 CONC on of Master Andrew sold Ochiltree to his cousin, Sir James Stewart within 10 years after 1605, so Andrew still held that title in 1605. Lord Andrew and Lady Margaret had a brother named James Stewart as given here in this profile
2 CONC . Scots Peerage VI appears to call him "John" rather than James. Then his son, Thomas, named his son John who named his successor John again. Two more Johns followed these Johns. The compromise is to call this first of the line:  
2 CONC James John Stewart. Like all the rest of his relatives in this family line, his wife's ancestry is very close to his own. She was born a Campbell and her father line leads quickly up to Lorn then Robert II Stewart, King of Scots a
2 CONC nd Robert I the Bruce, king of Scots. Her paternal grandmother's Gordons quickly lead to James I, King of Scots' wife, Joan Beaufort who was the granddaughter of King Edward III of England through his son Prince John of Gaunt and 
2 CONC Katherine, Duchess of Lancaster. James' wife's mother leads to Montgomery/Campbell, Lorn, Albany, to Robert II and Robert the Bruce two different ways.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Stewart 1585 – 1640
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Stewart 1590 – 1670
2 CONT --------------------
